    "id": "thm:han-strict-refinement",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Fix \\(k\\) with \\(p_k>0\\), and write \\(\\eta_k=p_kB_{kk}\\). Han--Owusu--Shin Appendix-B Eq. (S1) is exactly \\[\\widehat U^{HOS}_{C,k}=\\frac{1}{Cp_k}\\sum_{c=1}^C\\mathbf 1\\{L_c=k\\}\\frac{\\gamma_k(Z_c)}{\\beta_k(Z_c)}W_c(Z_c)=\\frac{1}{C\\eta_k}\\sum_{c=1}^C\\mathbf 1\\{L_c=k,Z_c\\in\\mathcal S_k\\}W_c(Z_c).\\] It has influence function \\(\\phi_k^{HOS}=\\eta_k^{-1}\\mathbf 1\\{L=k,Z\\in\\mathcal S_k\\}W(Z)-U_k\\) and \\[\\operatorname{AVAR}(\\widehat U^{HOS}_{C,k})=\\frac{\\tau_k^2+b_k^2}{\\eta_k}+U_k^2\\left(\\frac1{\\eta_k}-1\\right).\\] The best regular estimator in the nominal-label-\\(k\\) subexperiment has canonical gradient \\(\\phi_k^{nom}\\) and efficiency bound \\(\\tau_k^2/\\eta_k\\). Therefore, whenever \\(q_k>\\eta_k\\) and \\(\\tau_k^2>0\\), \\[\\frac{\\tau_k^2}{q_k}<\\frac{\\tau_k^2}{p_kB_{kk}}\\leq\\operatorname{AVAR}(\\widehat U^{HOS}_{C,k}).\\] If \\(b_k^2>0\\), assignment-cell calibration within the nominal subexperiment is itself strictly more efficient than Eq. (S1), apart from the additional Horvitz--Thompson participation term displayed above.",
